    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and educated by his uncle Justin, Justinian was the power behind the throne in Justin's last years and ruled alone from 527 to 565.</li><li>His generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> won back <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and his forces took southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, but the long Gothic war devastated Italy and only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> proved lasting gains.</li><li>His codification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> became the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> in many states, while his laws against pagans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a>, heretics and homosexual men show the harsh side of his drive for unity of faith.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the early 550s two monks smuggled silkworm eggs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> became a product of the empire itself.</p></aside>
    </section>

