    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">527</span><span class="rf-text">Justinian is crowned co-emperor on 1 April and becomes sole ruler after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-i">Justin I</a> dies on 1 August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">529</span><span class="rf-text">The first draft of the Codex Justinianeus is issued; the Neoplatonic Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> is closed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">532</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a> nearly overthrow him and end in a massacre in the Hippodrome; the Eternal Peace is made with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">533</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> at Ad Decimum and Tricamarum and takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">537</span><span class="rf-text">The rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> is completed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">540</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> enters Ravenna, while Khosrau I breaks the Eternal Peace and sacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">552</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> at Busta Gallorum, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> is killed; Spania is founded in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">565</span><span class="rf-text">Justinian dies and is succeeded by his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to his contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, Justinian was born in the hamlet of Tauresium in the province of Dardania, near the fortress of Bederiana; later scholarship places his birth around 482. Tauresium is most commonly identified with Gradište near the village of Taor, southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, though its distance from Justiniana Prima, the city he later founded nearby, has kept the location under debate. He came from a peasant family thought to be of Thraco-Roman or Illyro-Roman origin and was a native speaker of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, among the last Eastern Roman emperors to be one.</p><p>His mother, Vigilantia, was the sister of Justin, commander of one of the imperial guard units, the Excubitors. Justin adopted him, brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and had him educated in jurisprudence, theology and Roman history; the name Iustinianus reflects that adoption. Justinian served as a candidatus, one of 40 men chosen from the palace guards as the emperor's bodyguard. John Malalas described him as short, fair-skinned, curly-haired, round-faced and handsome; Procopius, in similar terms, compared him to the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>, perhaps an allusion to one of the most despised men in Roman history.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i">Theodora (wife of Justinian I)<span>Married</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When the emperor Anastasius died in 518, Justin was proclaimed emperor with considerable help from Justinian. Several sources claim that Justinian acted as virtual regent long before he was made associate emperor, though there is no conclusive evidence of this; as Justin became senile, Justinian became the ruler in fact. After the assassination of the general Vitalian in 520, orchestrated by Justinian or Justin, he was made consul and commander of the army of the east. In the church he helped end the Acacian schism with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> soon after 518. In 525 he received the titles of nobilissimus and caesar, marking him as heir.</p><p>Around 525 he married his mistress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i">Theodora</a>, an actress and prostitute some twenty years his junior; the marriage, made possible because Justin had lifted restrictions on marriages with ex-actresses, caused a scandal, but Theodora became very influential in politics. Justinian was crowned co-emperor on 1 April 527 and became sole ruler on Justin's death on 1 August 527. He was known as "the emperor who never sleeps". Around him were able men: Tribonian, his legal adviser; Peter the Patrician; the finance ministers John the Cappadocian and Peter Barsymes, who collected taxes more efficiently than any before them; and the generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Justinian had Tribonian revise the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, something never attempted before. The Codex Justinianeus appeared in draft on 7 April 529 and in final form in 534, the Digest in 533, then the Institutiones, a textbook, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novellae">Novellae</a>, new laws issued in Greek. Together they are known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>. His laws protected prostitutes and women forced into prostitution, returned dowries to widows, gave slaves the right to plead for their freedom and made killing a slave murder, and showed concern for abandoned and illegitimate children.</p><p>Having made the Eternal Peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 532 for 11,000 pounds of gold, he turned west. In 533 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> sailed with about 15,000 men, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. From 535 Belisarius took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and in 540 entered Ravenna; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a>'s revival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> with about 35,000 men won at Busta Gallorum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> Lactarius in 552 and defeated a Frankish invasion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casilinum">Casilinum</a> in 554. A force of 2,000 men took Cartagena and founded the province of Spania. The empire reached its territorial zenith in 555. At home he rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, completed in 537 under Isidore of Miletus and Anthemius of Tralles, the Church of the Holy Apostles and Saints Sergius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a>, and built fortifications, the Basilica Cistern, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakarya-river">Sangarius</a> Bridge and Justiniana Prima.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Justinian I&#8221; cites where it covers Legislative activities, Nika riots, Military activities, Religious activities:</p><ul><li>Anthony Kaldellis, The secret history: with related texts (Hackett Pub. Co, 2010)</li><li>James Allan Evans, The Emperor Justinian and the Byzantine Empire (Greenwood Press, 2005)</li><li>William Rosen, Justinian's Flea: Plague, Empire, and the Birth of Europe (Viking Adult, 2007)</li><li>J. A. S. Evans, The Age of Justinian: The Circumstances of Imperial Power (Routledge, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>His rule was not universally popular. In January 532 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>-racing factions united in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a>, forced the dismissal of Tribonian and two other ministers and tried to crown the senator Hypatius. Justinian considered flight but stayed, apparently at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i">Theodora</a>'s urging; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> and Mundus suppressed the revolt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> relates that 30,000 unarmed civilians were killed in the Hippodrome. A conspiracy against his life was discovered as late as 562. Procopius, who praised his buildings, wrote in the Secret History a hostile portrait of a cruel, corrupt and incompetent ruler. The heavy taxes laid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> were deeply resented, and some historians argue he dangerously stretched the empire's resources.</p><p>His drive for unity of faith under the Chalcedonian church brought persecution. Laws barred philosophers, astrologers, heretics, pagans, Manicheans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> from public office, destroyed their places of worship and restricted their property; pagan practice was restricted even in private, and the Samaritans, who repeatedly revolted, were forbidden to make wills in 529. Laws against male homosexual acts were applied retrospectively on a single witness, and those convicted were mutilated and paraded. His attempts to win over the Miaphysites satisfied none of the parties; the condemnation of the Three Chapters, to which Pope Vigilius was forced to assent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, caused a schism in the west.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The later reign was burdened by disaster. In 540 Khosrau I broke the Eternal Peace, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and forced Justinian to pay 5,000 pounds of gold and 500 more a year; the war ended with a fifty years' peace in 562 that required an annual Roman tribute for Lazica. The plague that struck in 541-543 afflicted the emperor himself, who recovered; it was traditionally believed to have killed tens of millions, though recent research argues the sources overstated it and scholars remain divided. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i">Theodora</a> died in 548, possibly of cancer. In 559 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a> and Sklavinoi under Zabergan threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and were repulsed by the aged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>.</p><p>In his last years Justinian grew isolated and relied on a small circle of advisers. He became increasingly fixated on religion and intolerant, yet less ruthless: after an earthquake in 557 he gave up wearing his crown and gave to the poor, and in the 560s he showed clemency to conspirators against him and pardoned Belisarius, accused of joining the plot. He died in his sleep on the night of 14 November 565, childless. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a>, married to Theodora's niece Sophia, was quickly acclaimed emperor, and Justinian was buried in a golden tomb in the Church of the Holy Apostles, which was robbed when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> pillaged the city in 1204.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a> ensured the survival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>. It underlay later Byzantine law in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilika">Basilika</a>, passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> in the 12th century to become the basis of much Continental law, spread with European empires to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; it is still the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> in many modern states. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> remained the centre of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> for centuries. Of his conquests only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> proved lasting, and much of Italy was lost soon after his death, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and Ravenna were kept. Some historians hold that Africa and southern Italy paid for themselves; Peter Sarris argues the plague made the reconquest harder and costlier.</p><p>Justinian is widely regarded as a saint by Eastern Christians and is commemorated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i">Theodora</a> on 14 November. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s Paradiso he appears as a spirit in the sphere of Mercury, a lawgiver and restorer of Rome who confesses that fame as well as duty moved him. Historians draw on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, whose Wars and Buildings are more favourable than his Secret History, and on the more neutral chronicle of John Malalas. Novelists from Robert Graves in Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> to Guy Gavriel Kay have portrayed him, often as jealous of his greatest general.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Justinian I ruled the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> from 527 to 565. His generals won back <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> at great cost, his jurists compiled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>, and he rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a>, persecution of religious minorities, war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and plague marked his reign.</em></p>

